  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜すぎる (sugiru) — too much",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe pattern Verb ます-stem (masu-stem) / Adjective stem + すぎる (sugiru) means \"too much ~\" or \"excessively ~.\" For い-adjectives (i-adjectives): drop い (i) + すぎる: 高い (takai) → 高すぎる (taka sugiru, \"too expensive\"). For な-adjectives (na-adjectives): stem + すぎる. すぎる (sugiru) itself conjugates as an ichidan verb.\n\nExample from this song:\n小さすぎて (chiisa sugite) — \"too small (so...)\"\nSomething is excessively small, leading to a consequence expressed in the next clause.\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べすぎた (tabe sugita) — \"ate too much\"\n• この映画は長すぎる (kono eiga wa naga sugiru) — \"this movie is too long\"\n• 静かすぎて怖い (shizuka sugite kowai) — \"it's so quiet it's scary\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "小さい (chiisai, \"small\") → 小さ (chiisa, stem) + すぎる (sugiru, \"too much\") → 小さすぎて (chiisa sugite, te-form, \"too small, so...\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ている / 〜てる (te iru / teru) — progressive / state",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe pattern Verb て-form (te-form) + いる (iru) expresses ongoing action or resulting state. In casual speech, the い (i) drops: ている (te iru) → てる (teru). This casual contraction is extremely common in everyday spoken Japanese, songs, and informal writing.\n\nExample from this song:\n泣いてる (naiteru) — \"is crying\" (casual)\nThe casual form captures the immediacy and rawness of the emotion.\n\nMore examples:\n• 何してる？ (nani shiteru?) — \"what are you doing?\" (casual)\n• もう知ってる (mou shitteru) — \"I already know\" (casual)\n• ずっと待ってた (zutto matteta) — \"had been waiting all along\" (casual past)"
      },
      "conjugation_path": "泣く (naku, \"to cry\") → 泣いて (naite, te-form) → 泣いている (naite iru, progressive) → 泣いてる (naiteru, casual, \"is crying\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜てく (teku) — going on doing (casual)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nてく (teku) is the casual contraction of ていく (te iku), which means an action continues or progresses into the future — moving away from the present. The い (i) is dropped for casual flow. This is extremely common in songs and casual speech.\n\nExample from this song:\nぶっとばしてく (buttobashiteku) — \"blasting away\" / \"keeps knocking out\"\nThe action will keep going forward with relentless energy.\n\nMore examples:\n• 変わってく (kawatteku) — \"keeps changing\"\n• 消えてく (kieteku) — \"fading away\"\n• 走ってく (hashitteku) — \"running off\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "ぶっとばす (buttobasu, \"to blast/knock out\") → ぶっとばして (buttobashite, te-form) + いく (iku) → ぶっとばしていく (buttobashite iku) → ぶっとばしてく (buttobashiteku, casual, \"keeps blasting away\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "何も〜ない (nani mo ... nai) — nothing / not anything",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N5",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe pattern 何も (nani mo) + negative verb means \"nothing\" or \"not anything.\" This is part of a broader pattern where question words + も (mo) + negative = total negation: 誰も〜ない (dare mo ... nai, \"nobody\"), どこも〜ない (doko mo ... nai, \"nowhere\"), いつも〜ない (itsu mo ... nai, \"never\"). This is one of the first patterns beginners learn.\n\nExample from this song:\n何もない (nani mo nai) — \"there is nothing\"\nA stark declaration of emptiness.\n\nMore examples:\n• 何も食べていない (nani mo tabete inai) — \"I haven't eaten anything\"\n• 何も心配しないで (nani mo shinpai shinaide) — \"don't worry about anything\"\n• 誰も来なかった (dare mo konakatta) — \"nobody came\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "何 (nani, \"what\") + も (mo) + ない (nai, negative) → 何もない (nani mo nai, \"there is nothing\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜けれど / 〜けど (keredo / kedo) — but / although",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nけれど (keredo) or its casual form けど (kedo) means \"but\" or \"although.\" It connects two contrasting clauses. The full form けれども (keredomo) is the most formal, けれど (keredo) is standard, and けど (kedo) is casual. It is one of the most common conjunctions in Japanese.\n\nExample from this song:\n無いけれど (nai keredo) — \"there isn't, but...\"\nDespite something being absent, there is still a contrasting reality.\n\nMore examples:\n• 高いけど美味しい (takai kedo oishii) — \"it's expensive but delicious\"\n• 行きたいけど時間がない (ikitai kedo jikan ga nai) — \"I want to go but don't have time\"\n• 分かってるけど (wakatteru kedo) — \"I understand, but...\" (trailing off)"
      },
      "conjugation_path": "無い (nai, \"there isn't\") + けれど (keredo, \"but\") → 無いけれど (nai keredo, \"there isn't, but...\")"
    }
  ]
}